On Dec 21, 10:56 pm, ais523 <ais...@nethack4.org> wrote:

> The whole "look at log to find details about bones" is a
> pretty interesting issue from a game design point of view;

Yes, I think it's well worth tossing around
a few ideas.

> it's not possible in vanilla (except by asking the player
> who died, or by finding your own bones and remembering

When you play on your own computer, it is inevitable that
you will find your own bones sometimes (unless you disable
bones, of course, but where's the fun in that?)  I've also
run into my own bones on NAO once, although I was NOT
particularly happy to see that level again.  (It was already
a bones level the first time I encountered it, and it
managed to kill me the second time as well.  I like to think
someone else eventually conquered it, but I don't know how
to find out.)

And I think that under the current system, where any given
level only has one bones file at a time (so bones are
generally a quite recent game, unless they are very deep),
it is relatively inevitable that anyone who finds his own
bones, especially in the early game, is going to remember
some stuff about the former game.  (Unless the player has a
rather bad memory, but I don't see how a person with a poor
memory could do very well at NetHack.  Remembering various
things is such an important survival skill in this game.)

One obvious partial solution is to allow multiple bones
files to exist at the same time for the same level.  (No
more than one would be *loaded* for any given level in any
given game, obviously.  If the game decides to give you
bones for a certain level, it would then decide at random
which ones to give you.)  This does not make the problem go
away entirely, but it introduces an additional uncertainty
factor and the possibility of more distant memories that
aren't as clear in one's mind.

> but the dumplogs definitely generally add to the
> game, so removing them would be a mistake.

I definitely agree with this.

> I've already taken steps towards reducing abuse in AceHack
> and NetHack 4 (items discovered in a bones pile will be
> sorted by alphabetical order of possibly unidentified name
> in the game encountering them,

That sounds like a good idea to me.  Again, it doesn't make
the issue go away entirely, but it mitigates it somewhat, in
a way that seems very in keeping with the NetHack way of
doing things in general and also does not significantly
reduce the usefullness or interest of dumplogs.

> I'd like to make bones further impossible to abuse along
> these lines, but it's not entirely clear how.

One idea that occurred to me, which would help somewhat in
the public-server scenario, is to randomize the name of the
ghost and on the tombstone of a bones level (pulling a name
e.g. from the high score file or somesuch), to make it
somewhat more difficult to identify the specific game that
the pile comes from.  Withholding the date might also be
effective, particularly in combination with the
aforementioned possibility of allowing more than one bones
file to accumulate for any given level so that the one you
get may not necessarily be the newest.  (In fact, taking
this a step further, bones files could even be set aside
when first created and allowed to age for a random number of
days before being pulled into the pool for possible use.)

An obvious downside of this (that is, of making it harder to
identify which game the bones you got came from) is that you
then cannot later easily look up the other game to see how
in the name of all that you hold dear the other player
managed to get the level into that state, and I think in
some cases curiousity might drive me mad.  It's bad enough
I don't know who got my bones or what happened to them.

A possible way to offset this downside would be to keep hold
of the original player's name and time of death and, at the
end of the game (whether you die or ascend or whatever)
offer to inform you about any bones files you encountered in
the game, so you can look them up then, when it's too late
to help you.

> (Or just encourage playing without bones;

I like bones.  They add a social aspect to the game
that I think it needs, especially in the absense of
a cooperative multiplayer mode.

> bones are pretty abusable even without dumplog looking,
> because the levels they're on are rarely particularly
> deadly, and the loot approximately doubles your
> character's selection of possessions.)

The mere fact of using stuff you find in bones piles isn't
abuse, IMO.  It's an intended feature of the game.

And bones levels CAN be deadly.  VERY deadly, sometimes.
(I believe I have already told the story about the Minetown
level containing a barbed devil names Slinky...)


